<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Overview</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Starting a business means making many decisions before you know whether they will work. How
    much should the product cost? How many customers do you need to break even? What happens if
    rent goes up? How much funding will you need?
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    SumoSum brings AI-assisted planning and financial modelling together. You describe the business
    in plain words, and SumoSum builds a forecast you can explore and adjust. I was the UI/UX
    designer, working in Figma on the product, the admin panel and the plans and billing flows. The
    product is live at
    <a href="https://sumosum.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
      >sumosum.com<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
    >.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    One idea guided the design: <strong>strategy made visible</strong>. Don’t just show people the
    numbers. Help them understand the decisions behind them.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">The problem</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Strategy is usually discussed in words, and financial planning is expressed in numbers, yet the
    two are tightly linked. A founder can have a clear idea of the business without knowing how
    pricing, customer acquisition, operating costs or sales volume will affect it.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Spreadsheet models are powerful, but they assume financial modelling skills many people don’t
    have. The challenge was not only to calculate the numbers but to help people understand what
    the numbers mean for their decisions.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    So I framed the work around one question: how might we make business strategy and financial
    modelling easier to understand, explore and act on, without burying people in financial
    complexity?
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Who it’s for</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">SumoSum serves people with very different levels of financial expertise:</p>
  <ul class="cs__p">
    <li>
      <strong>First-time entrepreneurs</strong> need a simple starting point, help turning an idea
      into a model, and a readable view of what might happen.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Accountants</strong> need a structured model where every assumption and output can be
      checked.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Business advisors</strong> need a way to explain financial consequences, test other
      scenarios and talk through a plan with a client.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The product had to be simple enough for a beginner without limiting an expert. That tension
    shaped most of the decisions below.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Five design decisions</h2>

  <h3 class="cs__h3">1. Start from the idea, not the spreadsheet</h3>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The home page opens with one question instead of a form full of financial fields. A single text
    box asks people to tell SumoSum what they’ll sell, at what price and in what quantity, and the
    AI builds the forecast from that. There is a microphone for people who would rather talk it
    through.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    People who think in a more structured way can switch to <strong>BMC Mode</strong>. It opens a
    Business Model Canvas with its nine blocks, from Key Partners to Revenue Streams, or lets them
    upload an existing canvas. Either way, the first step uses language founders already know.
  </p>
