  <h3 class="cs__h3">2. Make the AI understandable</h3>
  <p class="cs__p">
    A forecast that appears from nowhere is hard to trust. While SumoSum works, the builder shows
    each stage (reading the prompt, collecting real-time data, processing with AI, generating the
    forecast) with a progress bar and a step count.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Beside it, a <strong>Live Analysis</strong> panel restates the business in plain language: the
    products and prices, growth rate, team, cost of goods and major expenses the AI has picked up.
    People can see what the model will be built from and spot a misunderstanding before they reach
    the charts. I explored this screen in more than one colour treatment.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-builder.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1400"
      height="927"
      alt="SumoSum AI forecast builder at Step 9 of 10 and 89 percent progress. On the left, a robot mascot announces Added 1 service: Bookkeeping Service. On the right, a Live Analysis panel summarises the business in a paragraph above a checklist of four stages."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The forecast builder shows what the AI understood while it works.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="cs__h3">3. Keep assumptions next to their consequences</h3>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The dashboard is the centre of the product. It stacks two connected panels: purple
    <strong>Assumptions</strong> on top and red <strong>Charts &amp; Reports</strong> below. Change
    an input and the charts underneath recalculate, so the link between a decision and its
    financial effect stays on one screen.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Assumptions are split into eight categories so a model can be built in manageable pieces:
    Setup, Products, Assets, Staff, Marketing, Admin, Services and Funding. Each field has an info
    tooltip, and the panel has Footnotes and Comments for anyone reviewing the model, such as an
    accountant or advisor.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The Charts tab covers Cash Balance, Income Breakdown, Income vs Expenses, Cash Flow, Use of
    Funds and Burn Rate, with a time range and a relative-scale option. The Reports tab holds the
    profit and loss, cash flow statement and balance sheet, and Export turns the whole forecast into
    a report for sharing or pitching.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-charts.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1400"
      height="874"
      alt="Project dashboard for Buns Hawaii - Sandwich Truck, Base Case. A collapsed purple Assumptions bar sits above a red Charts and Reports panel with six charts: Cash Balance, Income Breakdown, Income vs Expenses, Cash Flow, Use of Funds and Burn Rate. A banner reads Save your Project - FREE! No credit card required."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The dashboard: assumptions above, the charts they drive below.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="cs__h3">4. Explain the charts, not just draw them</h3>
  <p class="cs__p">
    A chart shows an outcome but doesn’t say why it looks that way. I designed a 20-step guided tour
    that explains each chart, assumption category and tab in plain terms. Burn Rate, for example,
    “shows how quickly you’re using up your available funds” and helps a startup work out its
    runway. The tour can be skipped at any step, and every chart keeps an info icon for later.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/sumosum-assumptions.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1400"
      height="878"
      alt="Dashboard with the Assumptions panel open on its category tabs and fields such as Bank Account Interest, Credit Terms, Opening Cash Balance and Income Tax. A tour tooltip marked Step 8 of 20 reads Welcome to your forecasting workspace! with Skip and Next buttons."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The guided tour introduces the assumptions workspace, one step at a time.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 class="cs__h3">5. Let people try before they commit</h3>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Anyone can build a forecast as a guest. The project header invites them to save it, “FREE! No
    credit card required”, and the tour ends with a prompt to sign up so they don’t lose their
    progress. Value comes first and the account comes second.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Projects, scenarios and sharing</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    A business model is a working document, not a one-off file. Each project holds one or more
    scenarios, such as a base case and alternatives, so people can try a different price or cost
    structure without overwriting their first plan. A project with no scenarios points straight to
    Create Scenario.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The project list shows each person’s role and the number of members, and the sidebar adds My
    Groups, My Templates and a community Directory, which supports the shared way accountants and
    advisors work with business owners.
  </p>
