</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Beyond the core flow</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The Figma work also covers loading screens and a loading carousel that previews charts, reports
    and features while the AI works, alert screens, mobile and tablet layouts, the marketing site,
    and a WordPress plugin version of the single-input starter.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Design principles</h2>
  <ul class="cs__p">
    <li>
      <strong>Make complexity approachable.</strong> Break financial concepts into steps people
      already understand.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Connect inputs to outcomes.</strong> Keep every assumption near the result it changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Make AI understandable.</strong> Show what the AI is doing and what it understood.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Support exploration.</strong> Scenarios make it safe to ask “what if?”
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Design for every level of expertise.</strong> Start simple without capping the depth
      an expert needs.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Reflection</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Designing for business strategy and financial modelling takes more than arranging charts, forms
    and dashboard components. It means understanding how people think about decisions and how to
    show complex relationships without oversimplifying them.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The biggest lesson: a good strategy tool shouldn’t just produce an answer. It should help people
    understand the assumptions behind that answer and explore what happens when they change.
    Strategy is most useful when people can see it, question it and work with it.
  </p>
</section>
